## Follow-up results

### Diffuse population survives in a much wider joint-scaling window

The earlier TV-to-Haar condition `N kappa/M -> 0` is sufficient but not needed merely to preserve dense geometry. Let `X=N E_smooth`. Under Haar phases,

`E_0[X] <= A N kappa/(M+1)`

with certified branch-box coefficient `A<548.522`. Jensen gives

`Z=E_0[e^{-X}] >= exp(-E_0[X])`,

so for any event B,

`P_G(B) <= exp(E_0[X]) P_0(B)`.

Since `P_0(maxgap>delta)<=M(1-delta)^(M-1)`, the maximal gap still tends to zero for every fixed delta whenever

`N kappa=o(M^2)`.

Thus with fixed kappa, `q=M^3` and `N=M^alpha` for any `0<alpha<2` is compatible with sharp local phase concentration (`N->infinity`), vanishing phase collisions and dense RG-3 circle geometry. This proves geometric density, not TV convergence to Haar.

### Rewiring is local per crossing but has no size-uniform dwell time

A simple adjacent phase crossing changes only two cycle edges out and two edges in. However Haar circular spacings satisfy

`P(M^2 min_gap >= y)=(1-y/M)^(M-1) -> exp(-y)`.

Hence the smallest gap is typically `Theta(M^-2)`. With bounded angular velocity `V`, admissible trajectories can trigger a crossing on time scale `Theta((V M^2)^-1)`. There is no positive M-independent raw graph dwell time without an additional separation/dynamics premise.

### Operational influence metric

For a frozen RG-3 cycle, PHY-006 implies minimal discrete causal depth equals hop distance. Under iid Haar phases, DKW gives with probability at least `1-2 exp(-2M eps^2)`:

`|(2pi/M) d_hop - d_angle| <= 4pi eps + 2pi/M`

uniformly over sampled pairs. Thus normalized operational influence depth converges in probability to the intrinsic phase metric in the diffuse regime.

### Minimal exact metric backbone

If all circular gaps are `<pi`, the RG-3 cycle is not merely one convenient Delaunay choice: it is the unique inclusion-minimal graph with inherited phase distances whose shortest-path metric reproduces all sampled circle distances exactly. This removes one graph-choice ambiguity conditional on a zero-distortion/minimal-edge backbone principle.

### Refinement-compatible edge weights

For local quadratic edge energy `0.5*c(ell)(u-v)^2`, exact consistency under splitting `ell=a+b` and eliminating the inserted point forces

`c(a+b)=c(a)c(b)/(c(a)+c(b))`.

Therefore `R=1/c` is additive and, under regularity,

`c(ell)=kappa0/ell`.

The shape of the edge-weight law is thus unique up to one global positive scale. It transfers without retuning to arbitrary refinements.

### Conditional wave limit

If a local kinetic mass is also additive under interval refinement, `m(ell)=mu0 ell`. On a uniform mesh,

`omega(p)=2 sqrt(kappa0/mu0)|sin(ph/2)|/h`,

so `|v_g|<=sqrt(kappa0/mu0)` and the continuum limit is the one-dimensional wave equation with that dimensionless speed. This is a conditional hyperbolic model, not a derivation of physical `c`.

### Dimensional obstruction

All of the above geometry is sourced from one continuous phase `u in S1` plus a finite `Z2` fiber. Increasing q or M cannot raise the continuous topological dimension. A three-dimensional space requires additional independent relational coordinates or a different higher-dimensional orbit.
